<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#oto-manguean-languages">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oto-Manguean languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oto-Manguean languages are a large family of Indigenous languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, including Zapotec, Mixtec, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a>, Mazahua and Chinantec. They are tonal languages and among the oldest-attested language families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chorotega">Chorotega</a>, also called Mangue, spoken in Nicaragua and Costa Rica until it died out in the colonial era, was the family's southernmost member.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oto-manguean-languages.jpg" alt="Oto-Manguean languages" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noahedits · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Precontact_Otomanguean.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
